== The Preface

THE church of God has always acknowledged that the doctrine of #emph[original sin] is one of the fundamental truths of our Christian profession. It is, moreover, a special part of that particular possession of truth enjoyed by those whose religion toward God is built on divine revelation and rests finally on it. As the world by its #emph[wisdom] never knew God rightly, so its wise men were always utterly ignorant of this inborn evil in themselves and in others. With us, the #emph[doctrine] of it, and conviction of it, lie at the very foundation of all our dealings with God, both as to pleasing him here and as to obtaining the enjoyment of him hereafter. It is also known what bearing it has on the great truths concerning the #emph[person] of Christ, his mediation, its fruits and effects, and all the benefits we are made partakers of by it. Unless original sin is supposed, not one of these can be truly known or savingly believed. For this reason it has been treated at length by many holy and #emph[learned men], both in ancient and in more recent times. Some have labored to lay open #emph[its nature], some its #emph[guilt] and demerit; and they have also defended the #emph[truth] about it against the opposition made to it in past and present ages. Most have considered these things in their #emph[full extent] and breadth, as they concern all men by nature, together with the state and condition of those who are wholly under its power and guilt. Many have also fully shown how men are by it disabled and made incapable in themselves of giving the obedience required either in the law or in the gospel, so as to free themselves from the #emph[curse] of the one or to make themselves partakers of the #emph[blessing] of the other. Moreover, the Scripture abundantly testifies that #emph[remainders] of it abide in #emph[believers] after their regeneration and conversion to God, and this too has been fully taught and confirmed, as also how its #emph[guilt] is pardoned to them, and by what means its #emph[power] is weakened in them. All these things, I say, have been treated at length, to the great benefit and edification of the church. In what we now have in view, therefore, we take them all for #emph[granted], and only try to carry further the discovery of indwelling sin in its workings and its opposition to the #emph[law] and #emph[grace] of God in believers. Nor do I intend to discuss anything that has been disputed about it. What the #emph[Scripture] plainly reveals and teaches about it, what believers clearly find by #emph[experience] in themselves, and what they may learn from the examples and confessions of others, will be set out in a way suited to the capacity of the lowest and weakest who are concerned in it. And many things seem to make the handling of it at this time not unnecessary. Its effects and fruits, which we see in the apostasies and backslidings of many, the scandalous sins and failures of some, and the course and lives of most, seem to call for a due consideration of it. Besides, it will appear as we go on how much it concerns believers to have a full and clear acquaintance with the power of this #emph[indwelling sin] (the subject to be opened), to stir them up to watchfulness and diligence, to faith and prayer, and to call them to repentance, humility, and self-abasement. These in general were the ends aimed at in the discourse that follows. It was first composed and delivered for the use and benefit of a #emph[few], and is now by the providence of God made public. And if the #emph[reader] receives any benefit from these weak efforts, let him know that it is his duty both to give glory to God and to help with his prayers those who, in many temptations and afflictions, are willing to labor in the vineyard of the Lord, the work to which they are called.
